      "widgets_values": [
        "RTX VIDEO SUPER RESOLUTION \u2014 clean up an H3 take, don't redraw it\n\nWHAT IT IS, AND WHY IT SUITS THIS\n  NVIDIA's RTX VSR: the same engine as the Chrome/VLC driver feature, as a\n  ComfyUI node. It is NON-GENERATIVE. It upsamples, strips compression\n  artifacts and deblurs. It will not invent eyelashes.\n\n  That is the point here. H3 caps at 1.03 MP, so every take needs an upscale\n  pass -- and a GENERATIVE upscaler can redraw a face, which fights the\n  identity your references established. This one structurally cannot.\n\n  When you DO want detail invented, that is SEEDVR2 (7B, in models/SEEDVR2)\n  or FlashVSR. Different job, not a worse version of this one.\n\nSETTINGS\n  scale 2.0   640x1120 -> 1280x2240, or 768x1344 -> 1536x2688. The node caps\n              scale at 4.0 and snaps output to multiples of 8.\n  ULTRA       the node offers LOW/MEDIUM/HIGH/ULTRA.\n\n  THE SDK HAS MORE LEVELS THAN THE NODE SHOWS. nvvfx.effects.QualityLevel also\n  carries DEBLUR_LOW..ULTRA, DENOISE_LOW..ULTRA and HIGHBITRATE_LOW..ULTRA.\n  They are reachable in code, not wired to the dropdown. If ULTRA leaves\n  artifacts you would rather deblur or denoise specifically, that is where the\n  lever is -- a 4-line change in the node pack's __init__.py.\n\nAUDIO AND FRAME RATE SURVIVE, and that is wired deliberately\n  GetVideoComponents -> audio and fps both feed CreateVideo. H3 renders 24 fps\n  with its own generated soundtrack; leave the fps widget unwired and you\n  retime the take to 30 and lose the sound. The upstream example gets this\n  right and so does this one.\n\nMEMORY: IT ALLOCATES THE WHOLE OUTPUT UP FRONT\n  The node builds one tensor of (frames, H, W, 3) float32 before it starts.\n  Measured from the source, not guessed:\n\n      141 frames  640x1120 -> 1280x2240  =  4.5 GB\n      379 frames  640x1120 -> 1280x2240  = 12.1 GB\n      379 frames  768x1344 -> 1536x2688  = 17.5 GB\n\n  VRAM is NOT the constraint -- it batches to a 16.7 MP cap, so 4-5 frames per\n  GPU pass regardless of length. The cost is system RAM for the output buffer.\n\n  So a long take wants segmenting. Core's LoadVideo/VideoSlice work in SECONDS;\n  slice the take, upscale each piece, join the results. A 16-second 379-frame\n  take in one pass is 12 GB of float32 and worth splitting in two.\n\nWHERE IT RUNS\n  Ada and up -- the underlying tech goes back to RTX 20-series, despite the\n  FP4 talk in the announcements, which is a separate Blackwell feature.\n  Verified on this 4090: nvvfx imports, VideoSuperRes loads."
